GUI modernization, Phase 4 (fourth slice): the automated "no untagged
Latin sentence" scan - execution order P0-02's actual gate, ADR 0004's
last unimplemented test - plus a real, unplanned finding it led to.

## 1. What the scanner does and does not flag

`desktop/tests/visual/latin-text-scan.spec.ts` (new). The execution
order's exact wording: "an automated scan of the rendered Arabic app
that flags any Latin-script sentence not inside a `<bdi dir="ltr">`/
`TechnicalValue` wrapper." Built as a Playwright test reusing the
existing `openShell` helper (`tests/visual/helpers.ts`) rather than a
separate tool, since it needs a real rendered DOM the same way
`accessibility.spec.ts`'s axe scans do.

`sentenceLikeLatinRuns(text)`: finds every maximal run of Latin-script
words in a text node not excluded by ancestor (`bdi, code, pre, script,
style, [dir='ltr'], .ltr-field, .technical-value`), and flags a run only
if it has 3+ words AND contains at least one plain English function word
(the/is/which/from/...). This is deliberately not "any Latin text" - the
writing rules (execution order §10.4) keep short technical terms and
one-or-two-word parenthetical glosses inline in Latin script on purpose
("منفذ المبدّل (switch port)"), which is correct Arabic technical
writing, not a gap. A genuine leftover English sentence almost always
contains a function word; a technical noun, acronym or gloss almost
never does. Proven directly, not just asserted:

    proves the scanner itself catches an untranslated sentence, not
    just passes trivially
      clean:     "الوصلة منقطعة l2.neighbor_failed nrlab0 (LAN) VPN"        -> []
      dirty:     "النص: An interface went down. باقي الجملة"                -> ["An interface went down."]
      glossOnly: "حدّد منفذ المبدّل (switch port) الذي يتعطل"                -> []

## 2. What it found on first run, and how the exception list works

Every page (`overview, incidents, timeline, host, rules, evidence,
diagnostics, settings`) and all 6 wizard steps scanned clean except the
Incidents page and the wizard's sample-investigation step (which reuses
`IncidentCard` against the demo recording's longest-chain incident) -
192 flagged runs, all traced to one single, already-diagnosed cause:
`desktop/src/demo/demo-incidents.json` predates `Link.Clause`
(this session's third Phase 4 slice found this - see evidence 37), so
`rulesCatalogue.ts`'s `whyFor` correctly falls back to the original
English `why` on every chain link, exactly as designed against a fixture
it has no clause to translate from. Not a code defect: verified by
reading the actual flagged strings against the incident chain text
already captured in evidence 37 - every one matches.

Rather than weaken the scanner or blanket-exempt the Incidents page
(which would hide any *future*, *different* violation there too),
`KNOWN_GAPS` in the spec pins the **exact** 26 English sentences (+3 on
the wizard step) currently found. The assertion checks both directions:
anything beyond this exact list still fails (a new or different gap is
still caught), and every listed entry must still actually reproduce (if
the fixture is ever fixed, the now-stale entry fails the test too,
forcing its removal rather than letting a dead exception rot in place).
Both directions proven, not assumed: removed one entry, watched the
"unexpected violations" assertion fail with exactly that string; restored
it and confirmed all 3 tests pass again.

    $ npx playwright test latin-text-scan.spec.ts
    ok - proves the scanner itself catches an untranslated sentence...
    ok - the rendered Arabic app has no untagged Latin sentence on any page
    ok - the Arabic onboarding wizard has no untagged Latin sentence on any step
    3 passed

## 3. The unplanned finding: demo-incidents.json may itself be stale

Attempting to close the KNOWN_GAPS list for real (regenerate
`demo-incidents.json` with `Link.Clause` populated) surfaced something
bigger than an i18n gap. `internal/correlate/replaydemo/main.go` (new)
replays the unchanged `demo-events.json` (44 events, verified: no
duplicate `event_id`s, already sorted by `ts_wall`) through
`correlate.LoadRules` + `correlate.NewEngine(...).Offer(e)` per event, in
order - the same sequence `cmd/netrewindd/writer.go`'s `flush()` closure
runs live, minus the store (not needed: `Offer` returns the incidents
directly, and this tool only needs those, not persistence).

Running it produces **21 incidents, not the checked-in 16**:

    link-down-isolated-hosts: 2 -> 1        gateway-hijack:      1 -> 3
    contested-address:        1 -> 2        reachability-lost:   1 -> 2
    default-route-moved:      1 -> 2        default-route-lost:  1 -> 2
    service-unreachable:      2 -> 1        change-broke-a-path: 1 -> 2
    rogue-dhcp-server:        1 -> 2        port-flapping:       1 -> 0

Every other rule's count matched. This is a real, substantive divergence
between what the rules/engine concluded when this fixture was captured
and what they conclude now given the identical input - either a genuine
behavior change in `internal/correlate` since then (bug fix, regression,
or an intentional change never reflected back into the demo fixture) or
a gap in what this replay tool reproduces versus the live pipeline (fold/
dedup timing was the first thing checked and ruled out: the events file
has no duplicate IDs and is already time-ordered).

**Did not overwrite the checked-in fixture.** Restored it from a backup
immediately (`git status` on it confirmed clean) rather than trust
21-incidents output that could be silently wrong in either direction.
This is a genuinely separate question from rule/kind localization -
tracked as its own investigation task rather than chased down inside
this slice, per this execution order's own instruction not to let one
task's scope swallow an unrelated discovery. `replaydemo` is committed
as-is: a correct, working, but not-yet-applied tool, because building it
correctly is exactly what surfaced the finding, and it is what that
investigation will use.

